Here are a few clean ways to check if a property is null and only evaluate its value if it isn't null in C#:

1. Null Conditional Operator (?.):

```csharp
if(obj?.Property != null)
{
  // Property is not null, evaluate value
  if(obj.Property == someValue)
  {
    // do something
  }
}
```

2. Null Coalescing Operator (??): 

```csharp 
var property = obj.Property ?? defaultValue;
if(property == someValue)
{
  // do something
}
```

3. Conditional operator (?:):

```csharp
var property = obj.Property;
if(property != null && property == someValue)
{
  // do something 
}
```

4. Check for null before accessing:

```csharp
if(obj != null && obj.Property != null)
{
  if(obj.Property == someValue) 
  {
    // do something
  }
}
```

5. Use null propagation in a method:

```csharp
void ProcessProperty(SomeClass obj) 
{
  if(ProcessNonNullProperty(obj))
  {
    // do something 
  }
}

bool ProcessNonNullProperty(SomeClass obj)
{
  return obj?.Property == someValue;
}
```

So in summary, the null conditional operator, null coalescing operator and conditional operator are clean ways to check for null before evaluating a property value.

Sure, Model-View-ViewModel (MVVM) is a software architectural pattern that separates the development of graphical user interfaces into three interconnected components: the Model, which represents the data or the business logic; the View, which is the UI; and the ViewModel, which acts as an intermediary between the Model and the View.

Here's a simple example to illustrate MVVM in Flutter:

1. Model: This represents the data layer or business logic.

```dart
class User {
  String name;
  int age;

  User(this.name, this.age);
}
```

2. ViewModel: This is the intermediary that handles the business logic and communicates with the model.

```dart
import 'package:flutter/foundation.dart';
import 'user.dart';

class UserViewModel with ChangeNotifier {
  User _user;

  UserViewModel(this._user);

  String get userName => _user.name;
  int get userAge => _user.age;

  void updateUser(String name, int age) {
    _user.name = name;
    _user.age = age;
    notifyListeners();
  }
}
```

3. View: The UI that displays the data and notifies the ViewModel of user actions.

```dart
import 'package:flutter/material.dart';
import 'package:provider/provider.dart';
import 'user.dart';
import 'user_view_model.dart';

void main() {
  runApp(MyApp());
}

class MyApp extends StatelessWidget {
  @override
  Widget build(BuildContext context) {
    User user = User('John Doe', 30); // Example user data

    return MaterialApp(
      title: 'MVVM Example',
      home: ChangeNotifierProvider(
        create: (context) => UserViewModel(user),
        child: UserView(),
      ),
    );
  }
}

class UserView extends StatelessWidget {
  @override
  Widget build(BuildContext context) {
    UserViewModel viewModel = Provider.of<UserViewModel>(context);

    return Scaffold(
      appBar: AppBar(
        title: Text('MVVM Example'),
      ),
      body: Center(
        child: Column(
          mainAxisAlignment: MainAxisAlignment.center,
          children: <Widget>[
            Text('Name: ${viewModel.userName}'),
            Text('Age: ${viewModel.userAge}'),
            ElevatedButton(
              onPressed: () {
                viewModel.updateUser('Jane Doe', 25);
              },
              child: Text('Update User'),
            ),
          ],
        ),
      ),
    );
  }
}
```

In this example, `User` is the Model containing data, `UserViewModel` is the ViewModel that provides data to the UI and handles logic for updating the user, and `UserView` is the View that displays the user's information and contains a button for updating it. Notice how `ChangeNotifierProvider` from the `provider` package is used to provide an instance of `UserViewModel` to the `UserView`. When the user presses the button, the ViewModel updates the user's information, which causes the UI to rebuild with the new data.

Remember that this is a simplified example, and MVVM can be implemented in many different ways in Flutter, especially when it comes to state management. You might choose different packages or approaches depending on the complexity of your application and your team's preferences.

Here is an example C++ implementation of a onewire search algorithm:

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>

// OneWire search algorithm
void onewireSearch(std::vector<bool>& id) {
  bool bit;
  bool last_zero = false;

  id.clear();

  // Reset bus
  reset_bus();

  // Begin search
  while (true) {
    // Read bit
    bit = read_bit();

    if (last_zero) {
      // We previously got 0, now we got 1
      // Collision, write 1
      write_bit(true);

      // Append 1 to ID
      id.push_back(true);
      last_zero = false;
    } else if (!bit) { 
      // We got 0
      // Write 0
      write_bit(false);

      // Append 0 to ID
      id.push_back(false);
      last_zero = true;
    } else {
      // Bus shorted or search done
      if (id.size() == 64) {
        // Search done, all 64 bits read
        break;
      } else {
        // Reset bus and restart search
        reset_bus();
        id.clear();
        last_zero = false; 
      }
    }
  }
}
```

The key ideas:

- Read a bit and write back its complement
- If two devices output different bits, there is a collision
- Append each bit to the device ID vector
- Detect bus shorts and restart if needed
- Stop when 64-bit ID is read

The reset_bus(), read_bit(), and write_bit() functions handle the actual 1-Wire communication. This implements the core search algorithm.

