Certainly! Below is an example of a simple HTML page with a button that, when clicked, changes the background color to a random pastel color. In this code, JavaScript is used to generate a random pastel color by altering the RGB values.

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
<meta charset="UTF-8">
<meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
<title>Random Pastel Color Background</title>
<style>
  body {
    display: flex;
    justify-content: center;
    align-items: center;
    height: 100vh;
    margin: 0;
    transition: background-color 0.5s; /* Smooth transition for color change */
  }
  .color-changer-btn {
    padding: 10px 20px;
    font-size: 16px;
    cursor: pointer;
  }
</style>
</head>
<body>
<button class="color-changer-btn" id="colorChanger">Change Background Color</button>

<script>
  function getRandomPastelColor() {
    // Generate random light colors (pastels)
    const base = 256;
    const offset = 127; // Adjust this to make colors lighter or darker
    const r = Math.floor(Math.random() * base) / 2 + offset;
    const g = Math.floor(Math.random() * base) / 2 + offset;
    const b = Math.floor(Math.random() * base) / 2 + offset;
    return `rgb(${r}, ${g}, ${b})`;
  }

  const button = document.getElementById('colorChanger');
  button.addEventListener('click', function() {
    document.body.style.backgroundColor = getRandomPastelColor();
  });
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

Here's how the code works:

- The CSS styles the body of the HTML to be a full vertical height and centers the button.
- The transition property in the body's style provides a smooth color transition effect.
- The JavaScript function `getRandomPastelColor()` generates a pastel color by mixing RGB with high values (for lightness) and some randomness.
- The button with id `colorChanger` listens for a click event and changes the body's background color using the generated pastel color.

You can copy this code into an HTML file and open it in a web browser to see the effect.

Here is a basic 2D platformer game in Unity:

```csharp
// Player controller
public class PlayerController : MonoBehaviour
{
    public float speed = 5f;
    public float jumpSpeed = 8f;
    public float gravity = 20f;

    private Rigidbody2D rb;
    private Animator anim;
    private bool isGrounded;

    void Start()
    {
        rb = GetComponent<Rigidbody2D>();
        anim = GetComponent<Animator>();
    }

    void Update()
    {
        // Get input and move horizontally
        float xInput = Input.GetAxis("Horizontal");
        rb.velocity = new Vector2(xInput * speed, rb.velocity.y);

        // Flip player sprite based on direction
        if (xInput > 0) 
        {
            transform.eulerAngles = new Vector3(0, 0, 0);
        }
        else if (xInput < 0) 
        {
            transform.eulerAngles = new Vector3(0, 180, 0); 
        }

        // Jump
        if (Input.GetButtonDown("Jump") && isGrounded)
        {
            rb.velocity = new Vector2(rb.velocity.x, jumpSpeed);
            isGrounded = false;
        }

        // Play walking animation
        anim.SetFloat("Speed", Mathf.Abs(xInput));
    }

    void FixedUpdate()
    {
        // Apply gravity
        rb.velocity = new Vector2(rb.velocity.x, rb.velocity.y - gravity * Time.fixedDeltaTime);
    }

    void OnCollisionEnter2D(Collision2D col)
    {
        // Set isGrounded to true if player collides with ground layer
        if (col.gameObject.layer == LayerMask.NameToLayer("Ground"))
        {
            isGrounded = true;
        }
    }
}
```

This script handles:

- Moving the player horizontally with input
- Jumping when the player presses the jump button and is grounded
- Applying gravity
- Flipping the sprite based on direction
- Playing a walking animation

You would also need:

- A tilemap for the level
- Colliders on the ground tiles 
- An animator with walking animations
- A sprite for the player
- A camera to follow the player

This covers the basics of a simple 2D platformer in Unity. Let me know if you have any other questions!

