Here are a few common ways to convert a potential undefined value to a string in JavaScript:

- Use toString():

  ```js
  const value = someVariable;
  const str = value.toString();
  ```

  This will return 'undefined' if value is undefined.

- Use String():

  ```js 
  const value = someVariable;
  const str = String(value);
  ```

  Same as above, returns 'undefined' if value is undefined.

- Check for undefined first:

  ```js
  const value = someVariable;
  let str;

  if(value !== undefined) {
    str = value;
  } else {
    str = 'undefined'; 
  }
  ```

- Short-circuit OR (||) operator:

  ```js
  const value = someVariable;
  const str = value || 'undefined';
  ```

  If value is falsy like undefined, it will return 'undefined'.

- Nullish coalescing operator (??) in modern environments:

  ```js
  const value = someVariable;
  const str = value ?? 'undefined'; 
  ```

  Returns 'undefined' only if value is nullish (null or undefined).

So in summary, toString(), String(), ||, and ?? are some common safe ways to convert an undefined value to a string in JavaScript.

